# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Four Fingers Cross the Grade Line

**Story & Context:** The debut opens at 160 BPM with Lin repeating one Dorian pipa note using four distinct right-hand densities. Marcus assigns a different footwork response to each state: single pluck receives no kick, short roll receives one displaced kick, full tremolo causes the hats to disappear, and palm mute is answered by a three-hit kick cluster. The tonal center never changes for the first thirty-two bars, but the perceived rhythmic speed changes constantly.

Zhou enters every eighth bar with one damped marimba fifth. Dana waits until Lin moves into the upper register, then places a dry bass-oboe fundamental beneath the next two cycles. Chen uses one woodblock stroke at the exact moment Lin changes from roll to tremolo, making the transition physically obvious without a riser or fill.

At the midpoint, Lin maintains a continuous tremolo while Marcus removes nearly all electronic high percussion. The pipa alone supplies the rapid top-end motion. The final cycle ends with a hard palm mute but Marcus deliberately withholds the expected kick cluster, leaving the warehouse room tone exposed.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close overhead image of a pipa right hand with four fingers extended above the strings, four cobalt grading marks drawn across cream porcelain sample cards, drum-machine steps and one damped marimba bar in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “four fingers,” “cross the line,” “hold the pitch,” and “mute before the cluster.”

## Track 2: Cobalt Tile Lands on Beat Three-and-a-Half

**Story & Context:** Marcus builds the EP’s most disorienting groove at 164 BPM, placing a recurring kick on beat three-and-a-half while Lin plays a two-note pipa cell that appears to resolve on beat four. Chen taps a small cobalt test tile at the off-position every fourth bar, reinforcing the displaced accent without adding another drum-machine layer.

Zhou uses two prepared marimba notes a minor second apart, striking the lower pitch only after Lin’s tremolo has stopped. Dana plays breath-heavy bass-oboe semitone falls in the opposite register direction to the pipa. The combination creates a narrow vertical harmonic field while the rhythm keeps shifting around it.

Halfway through, Marcus removes the recurring displaced kick but leaves the tile tap in place. The listener continues to anticipate the missing low hit. When the kick finally returns sixteen bars later, Lin changes from tremolo to isolated plucks, making the groove feel slower even though the BPM has not moved.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Single cobalt-blue porcelain test tile resting at an angle across a hand-drawn 4/4 grid, its corner touching the mark labeled “3½,” pipa string and bass-oboe key crossing the frame, dry fluorescent warehouse light. Spoken fragments limited to “three and a half,” “tile first,” “kick missing,” and “do not correct the grid.”

## Track 3: Tremolo Stops, Conveyor Keeps Counting

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 156 BPM with a mechanical packing-conveyor click recorded from the old warehouse, reduced to one dry pulse every two beats. Lin plays continuous high-register pipa tremolo above a sparse footwork pattern while Marcus keeps the kick unusually restrained. The tension comes from waiting for the hand to stop.

Every time Lin palm-mutes the strings, Zhou answers with one low prepared-marimba octave and Dana releases a short bass-oboe tone. Chen adds no tile percussion until the third mute, where one small ceramic click replaces a snare hit completely.

In the final minute, Marcus removes the drum machine layer by layer until only the conveyor pulse remains. Lin gradually reduces tremolo density from rapid multi-finger rotation to two-finger alternation, then isolated single notes. Her last note stops completely while the conveyor keeps clicking for four more cycles. The recording ends before the machine itself stops.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Long empty porcelain packing conveyor disappearing into darkness, pipa resting on a grading table beside a mechanical counter, one cream tile and one cobalt pencil mark under cold fluorescent light. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “keep counting,” “hand stopped,” “conveyor still moving,” and “leave four cycles.”

